== Commit Policy ==
Commit after every meaningful unit of work — a function implemented, a bug fixed, a test added.
Each commit message should describe WHAT was done and WHY, not just "wip" or "changes".
Good: "feat: add input validation for player deposit amount"
Bad:  "wip", "fix", "changes", "update code"

This protects progress if the session is interrupted. On resume, git log tells the next coder
exactly what was already done, so nothing needs to be re-discovered or re-implemented.

== Workflow ==
{{- if .SkippedDesigner}}
1. Read feature YAML ({{.Feature.ID}}.yaml) — this is your spec
{{- else if .TaskBrief}}
1. Review the Task Brief above, then read acceptance-criteria.md and test-strategy.yaml (paths listed in "Acceptance Criteria & Test Strategy" above)
{{- else}}
1. Read task-brief.md, then read acceptance-criteria.md and test-strategy.yaml (paths listed in "Acceptance Criteria & Test Strategy" above)
{{- end}}
2. Check for prior progress — a previous session may have been interrupted:
   - Run: git log --oneline -20 — commits are the source of truth for what code is already done
   - Run: git diff — uncommitted changes may be leftover from the interrupted session
   - Check if coder-report.md already exists with partial content
   - If commits exist, review them to understand what's done; implement only what remains
   - If coder-report.md exists but is incomplete, continue from where it stopped
3. Implement remaining changes — commit after each meaningful step (see Commit Policy above)
4. Run guardrail check: ${FOURX_BIN:-4x} check {{.Feature.ID}}
   - `4x check` now runs build + lint automatically in coding phase
   - If it fails, read the error output, fix the issues, and re-run until it passes
   - Do NOT write coder-report.md until `4x check` passes
5. Run verify commands
6. Write or update coder-report.md with: changed files, build/test results, summary

